# NOT a missing edge — a DIFFERENT ANCHOR. Both edges are in the graph; the two sides disagree
# about which method to hang them on, and only for a function value in a FIELD INITIALIZER.
#
#   private final Function<String,String> initField = p -> readInit(p);
#   private final Function<String,String> ref       = this::readRef;
#
# javac compiles a field initializer into the constructor, so the bytecode's lexical container is
# <init> and the oracle folds the body there. The engine anchors a function value's calls at the
# sites that INVOKE it, and emits:
#
#   Asset#handleInit(String) -> Asset#readInit(String)
#   Asset#handleRef(String)  -> Asset#readRef(String)
#
# which is the more useful answer for a reachability consumer and is present in actual.edges. The
# lambdas assigned in the constructor BODY agree on both sides (Asset#<init> -> read / readBlock /
# audit), so this is specifically the field-initializer anchor, not function values in general.
#
# Listed rather than normalised away because the divergence is real and should stay visible: if a
# future change makes the engine also anchor at <init>, these lines start passing and the suite
# says so.
Asset#<init> -> Asset#readInit(String)
Asset#<init> -> Asset#readRef(String)
